  "meta": {
    "title": "Even number of a’s and even number of b’s",
    "blurb": "Two parity checks run side by side: the four states are the pairs (parity of a, parity of b). The product construction, drawn as a square — a reads horizontally, b vertically.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε"
      },
      {
        "w": "aa",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "abab",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabbabba",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "a",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "ab",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "aab",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "bbbaa",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "parity",
        "product-construction",
        "textbook"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "Each of \"even number of a's\" and \"even number of b's\" needs a two-state DFA. Running both at once needs a state for every *pair* of their states — 2 × 2 = 4. This is the **product construction**, the proof that regular languages are closed under intersection.\n\nChange which corners accept and the same four states recognise any Boolean combination of the two conditions: union (three corners), symmetric difference (the two off-diagonal corners), and so on."
    }
  }
}
